---
import "../styles/home.css";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const docs = (slug: string) => `${base}${slug}`.replace(/\/{2,}/g, "/");
const productRepo = "https://github.com/felipemm/spice-cdn";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Spice CDN — GitOps platform for Spice.ai on Kubernetes</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Run Spice.ai on Kubernetes with a GitOps control plane: one-command Kind installer, Argo CD, Vault secrets, and a web UI to manage instances in your own GitOps repo."
    />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href={`${base}favicon.svg`} />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@400;500;600;700&display=swap"
      rel="stylesheet"
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="site-header">
      <div class="shell">
        <a class="brand" href={base}>
          <span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          Spice CDN
        </a>
        <nav class="site-nav" aria-label="Primary">
          <a href="#features">Features</a>
          <a href="#how-it-works">How it works</a>
          <a href={docs("guides/user-guide/")}>Docs</a>
          <a href={docs("guides/install/")} class="nav-cta">Get started</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="hero shell">
        <p class="eyebrow">Open platform · Kind-first · GitOps-native</p>
        <h1>Spice.ai on Kubernetes, owned by your GitOps repo</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          Spice CDN is a <strong>product platform</strong> that ships a control-plane UI, Helm charts, and a
          release-pinned installer. You keep a <strong>separate GitOps repository</strong> as the source of truth —
          Argo CD reconciles instances, secrets, and upgrades without coupling your org to upstream product code.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href={docs("guides/install/")}>Install guide</a>
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href={docs("guides/architecture/")}>Architecture</a>
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href={productRepo} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>
        </div>
        <div class="install-block">
          <p class="install-label">Quick install</p>
          <div class="install-row">
            <pre id="install-cmd" aria-label="Install command">curl -fsSL …/install.sh | bash</pre>
            <button type="button" class="copy-btn" id="copy-install" aria-label="Copy install command">
              Copy
            </button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section shell" id="product">
        <div class="section-header">
          <h2>What is Spice CDN?</h2>
          <p>
            A batteries-included way to operate <a href="https://spiceai.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >Spice.ai</a
            > workloads on Kubernetes with enterprise-style delivery: Git-backed config, continuous sync, and secrets
            from Vault.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="split">
          <div class="prose">
            <h3>The problem it solves</h3>
            <p>
              Running data platforms in Kubernetes usually means stitching charts, ingress, secrets, and deployment
              workflows yourself. Spice CDN packages those concerns into a repeatable lab and production-shaped path:
              bootstrap a cluster, materialize a GitOps tree, push to <em>your</em> repo, and manage Spice instances from
              a web UI.
            </p>
            <h3>Two repositories, one clear boundary</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Product repo</strong> — application source, charts, <code>install.sh</code>, CI, and this docs site.</li>
              <li><strong>Your GitOps repo</strong> — what Argo CD syncs: <code>instances/*</code>, platform apps, and pinned chart copies.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Upgrades download a new release tarball; your instance YAML and org policies stay in GitOps.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="diagram" id="how-it-works">
            <p style="margin: 0 0 1rem; font-weight: 600; color: var(--fg)">End-to-end flow</p>
            <div class="flow">
              <div class="flow-step">
                <span class="flow-num">1</span>
                <span>Create an empty GitOps repo + PAT with <code>repo</code> scope</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div>
              <div class="flow-step">
                <span class="flow-num">2</span>
                <span
                  ><code>install.sh</code> downloads a pinned release and materializes charts + Argo manifests into a
                  local tree</span
                >
              </div>
              <div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div>
              <div class="flow-step">
                <span class="flow-num">3</span>
                <span>Kind cluster + ingress, Vault, External Secrets, Argo CD bootstrap</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div>
              <div class="flow-step">
                <span class="flow-num">4</span>
                <span>Push GitOps → Argo syncs control plane and Spice instances</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div>
              <div class="flow-step">
                <span class="flow-num">5</span>
                <span>Control plane writes instance YAML via Git API; cluster reconciles automatically</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="stack-row" aria-label="Platform stack">
          <span class="pill">Kind</span>
          <span class="pill">Argo CD</span>
          <span class="pill">Vault</span>
          <span class="pill">External Secrets</span>
          <span class="pill">ingress-nginx</span>
          <span class="pill">Helm</span>
          <span class="pill">Next.js control plane</span>
          <span class="pill">Spice.ai</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section shell" id="features">
        <div class="section-header">
          <h2>Main features</h2>
          <p>Everything you need to go from zero to queryable Spice endpoints — with ops patterns built in.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-grid">
          <article class="feature-card">
            <div class="feature-icon" aria-hidden="true">⚡</div>
            <h3>One-command installer</h3>
            <p>
              <code>curl … | bash</code> pulls a GitHub Release tarball, substitutes GitOps placeholders, and bootstraps
              a Kind lab. Override version with <code>SPICE_RELEASE</code> or upgrade with <code>install.sh --upgrade</code>.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class="feature-card">
            <div class="feature-icon" aria-hidden="true">🎛️</div>
            <h3>Control plane UI</h3>
            <p>
              Next.js app to list, create, and edit Spice instances. Changes commit to folders under
              <code>instances/&lt;name&gt;/</code> in your GitOps repo via the GitHub Contents API (Gitea supported for
              local labs).
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class="feature-card">
            <div class="feature-icon" aria-hidden="true">🔄</div>
            <h3>GitOps continuous delivery</h3>
            <p>
              Argo CD ApplicationSet deploys the control plane and each Spice instance from the same GitOps repository
              Argo never reads the product repo directly.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class="feature-card">
            <div class="feature-icon" aria-hidden="true">🔐</div>
            <h3>Vault &amp; External Secrets</h3>
            <p>
              Dev-mode Vault plus ESO wire dataset credentials from Vault KV into Kubernetes Secrets consumed by Spice
              pods — no secrets in Git.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class="feature-card">
            <div class="feature-icon" aria-hidden="true">📦</div>
            <h3>Helm charts included</h3>
            <p>
              <code>charts/spice-instance</code> wraps upstream Spice on Kubernetes; <code>deploy/helm/control-plane</code>
              ships the UI, optional MCP sidecar, cost views, and Superset integration hooks.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class="feature-card">
            <div class="feature-icon" aria-hidden="true">📊</div>
            <h3>Observability &amp; BI (optional)</h3>
            <p>
              Add-on templates for OpenCost budgets, Grafana/Prometheus stack, and Apache Superset SQL Lab databases
              per instance — enabled when you need them in GitOps.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class="feature-card">
            <div class="feature-icon" aria-hidden="true">🤖</div>
            <h3>MCP sidecar</h3>
            <p>
              Optional FastMCP server exposes platform operations to AI assistants — manage instances and cluster context
              from compatible MCP clients.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class="feature-card">
            <div class="feature-icon" aria-hidden="true">✅</div>
            <h3>Validated examples &amp; CI</h3>
            <p>
              <code>examples/instances/</code> are linted in CI; copy patterns for Databricks, S3, and local dev without
              guessing required labels like <code>ownerLayerSlug</code>.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section shell" id="get-started">
        <div class="section-header">
          <h2>Get started in minutes</h2>
          <p>Three steps after you have Docker, Kind, and an empty GitOps repository.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="steps">
          <div class="step-card">
            <strong>1</strong>
            <span
              >Create an empty GitHub (or Gitea) GitOps repo and a token with <code>repo</code> / contents write access.</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="step-card">
            <strong>2</strong>
            <span
              >Run the installer — interactive prompts or set <code>GITOPS_REPO_URL</code>, <code>GITOPS_PAT</code>, and
              <code>YES=1</code> for automation.</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="step-card">
            <strong>3</strong>
            <span
              >Push the materialized tree, sync <code>platform-gitops</code> in Argo CD, then open the control plane and
              create your first Spice instance.</span
            >
          </div>
        </div>
        <p style="text-align: center; margin-top: 2rem">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href={docs("guides/user-guide/")}>Read the full user guide</a>
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer class="site-footer">
      <div class="shell">
        <p>
          <a href={productRepo}>felipemm/spice-cdn</a> · Apache 2.0 · Built with Astro + Starlight
        </p>
        <p>
          Deploying to GitHub Pages? Set <code>PUBLIC_ASTRO_SITE</code> and <code>PUBLIC_ASTRO_BASE</code> at build time
          for project-site paths.
        </p>
      </div>
    </footer>

    <script
      define:vars={{
        baseUrl: import.meta.env.BASE_URL,
      }}
    >
      const pre = document.getElementById("install-cmd");
      const copyBtn = document.getElementById("copy-install");
      if (pre) {
        const b = (baseUrl || "/").replace(/\/$/, "");
        const cmd = `curl -fsSL ${window.location.origin}${b}/install.sh | bash`;
        pre.textContent = cmd;
        copyBtn?.addEventListener("click", async () => {
          try {
            await navigator.clipboard.writeText(cmd);
            copyBtn.textContent = "Copied!";
            copyBtn.classList.add("copied");
            setTimeout(() => {
              copyBtn.textContent = "Copy";
              copyBtn.classList.remove("copied");
            }, 2000);
          } catch {
            copyBtn.textContent = "Select & copy";
          }
        });
      }
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
